<h1>Video 076 — throws keyword</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Exception Handling Part-6 || throws keyword</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/076-ogOy-dU8AmY.txt). ASR decoded in place: thros / ths / throw keyword (when the topic is this video) = throws; checkered / Checker = checked; unchecker = unchecked; color / cal / caller method = caller; wired / wide / vid / w main = void main; string array access / asks / a = String[] args; abc.xt / abcx = abc.txt; Java.Langa / Langin interrupted = java.lang.InterruptedException; throws I / internet explorer (joke) = throws InterruptedException; do stop / do stuff = doStuff; do more stop = doMoreStuff; JBM / RVM = JVM; legend = listen; keep subing = keep writing; compar / compeller / compile a method = compiler / compile-time error; SOP / s of / outprint ln = System.out.println; ibo / I go exception = IOException; 100% paka = 100% pakka (sure); poker / mah babu / Mahesh papa = Telugu film Pokiri / Mahesh Babu; musta be cut = must be caught; yet Test.main = at Test.main.</p>
<p>He opens by promising the difference between `throw` and `throws` “at last.” He never delivers that comparison in these 99 minutes. This video is only `throws`: purpose, two ways to satisfy the compiler, a three-method delegation chain, three conclusions, then four cases (class/constructor/method; throwable types; throw new Exception vs throw new Error; five try-catch types and the fully-checked “never thrown” CE).</p>
<h3>00:06 — Last session was `throw`; this session is `throws`</h3>
<p>Observe: last session covered the `throw` keyword. This session is `throws` — he calls it the most valuable concept of this part.</p>
<p>Classroom line: “Sir, can you tell what is the difference between throw and throws?” He says there is a big difference, there is a chance they will ask it in the entire interview room, but do not answer yet. He will summarize throw vs throws at last. First you must know the purpose of throws. This is easy if you already know checked vs unchecked — that terminology comes here.</p>
<h3>00:36 — Do not start with `throw` vs `throws`</h3>
<p>He repeats: last part was throw; this part is throws. Difference later. Purpose first.</p>
<h3>01:18 — Example 1 on the board: `PrintWriter` writing hello to `abc.txt`</h3>
<p>Class Test, public static void main(String[] args). Create a PrintWriter for file abc.txt and write &quot;hello&quot;. PrintWriter is in java.io — he adds the import so nobody thinks the only problem is a missing import.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.PrintWriter;

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(&quot;abc.txt&quot;);
        pw.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
        // CE: unreported exception java.io.FileNotFoundException;
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
}</pre>
<h3>02:10 — Ask the compiler: valid or invalid?</h3>
<p>If you ask the compiler to compile this, the compiler says: I cannot. You are trying to write &quot;hello&quot; to abc.txt. At runtime that file may not be available. There may be a chance of `FileNotFoundException`, which is a checked exception. If that checked exception occurs, how are you handling it? Tell me, then only I can compile.</p>
<p>It does not mean FileNotFoundException will occur. It means there is a possibility. In our program, if there is any chance of raising a checked exception, compulsory we should handle it — either by try-catch or by `throws`. If we are not handling, the code will not compile.</p>
<p>Exact compiler message he writes (and later shows from javac):</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.PrintWriter;

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(&quot;abc.txt&quot;); // CE here
        pw.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
    }
}
// javac Test.java
// unreported exception java.io.FileNotFoundException
// must be caught or declared to be thrown</pre>
<h3>03:22 — “Must be caught or declared to be thrown”</h3>
<p>“Must be caught” = try-catch. “Declared to be thrown” = throws. That phrase is the compile-time error. By default you get it for this code.</p>
<p>He stresses: possibility to rise ≠ it occurs. Possibility is enough for the compiler.</p>
<h3>04:39 — Live `javac Test.java` for example 1</h3>
<p>He types the import, PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(&quot;abc.txt&quot;), println(&quot;hello&quot;), then:</p>
<p>javac Test.java</p>
<p>You didn’t report about this exception. unreported exception java.io.FileNotFoundException must be caught or declared to be thrown. Compile-time error.</p>
<p>Same rule again: if there is a possibility of raising a checked exception, compulsory we should handle it. How we handle, later. Otherwise the code will not compile. Clear for all.</p>
<h3>05:37 — Board dictation: the checked-exception rule (keep writing)</h3>
<p>He puts this under the `throws` keyword heading. Keep writing. He repeats the sentence many times so the room copies it.</p>
<p>In our program, if there is a possibility of raising a checked exception, then compulsory we should handle that checked exception. Otherwise we will get compile-time error saying:</p>
<p>unreported exception XXX must be caught or declared to be thrown</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board rule (not a full program by itself):
// possibility of checked exception
//   → handle (try-catch  OR  throws)
//   → else CE: unreported exception X must be caught or declared to be thrown</pre>
<h3>07:16 — Example 1 labelled on the board</h3>
<p>Two examples will be discussed. This is example one.</p>
<p>Possibility of FileNotFoundException (checked). We did not handle. Code will not compile.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.PrintWriter;

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(&quot;abc.txt&quot;);
        pw.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
        // CE: unreported exception java.io.FileNotFoundException
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
}</pre>
<p>Any explanation required? Make sure you have clear clarity. Example one done.</p>
<h3>08:53 — Example 2: `Thread.sleep(10000)`</h3>
<p>Same type of problem. Class Test, main, the thread wants to sleep for 10 seconds → Thread.sleep(10000) milliseconds.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
        // CE: unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
}</pre>
<h3>09:44 — Compiler: I cannot compile `sleep`</h3>
<p>Whenever you call sleep, the main thread may enter sleeping state. If main is sleeping, other threads may interrupt the main thread. There may be a chance of `InterruptedException`, which is checked. You did not report anywhere about that exception. Then how can I compile?</p>
<p>Import is not required (java.lang).</p>
<h3>11:23 — Live compile of example 2</h3>
<p>He replaces the PrintWriter code with Thread.sleep(10000). Same CE:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}
// javac Test.java
// unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException
// must be caught or declared to be thrown</pre>
<h3>11:59 — Example 2 on the board (keep writing)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
        // CE: unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
}</pre>
<h3>13:08 — Compiler already gives two solutions</h3>
<p>“Compiler only providing the solution — in how many ways we can handle?” Look at the message:</p>
<ol><li>must be caught</li></ol>
<ol><li>or declared to be thrown</li></ol>
<p>So this compile-time error can be handled in two ways:</p>
<ul><li>First way: try-catch (you already know this — handling the exception yourself)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Second way: throws keyword (purpose explained next)</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// Two ways to handle the CE from Thread.sleep / PrintWriter:
// 1. try-catch          → must be caught
// 2. throws SomeException → declared to be thrown</pre>
<h3>14:20 — First way: try-catch (compiler checks only that catch exists)</h3>
<p>Because sleep is the problem, wrap it in try / catch (InterruptedException e). End of main, end of class.</p>
<p>Responsibility of the compiler: check whether you wrote try-catch or not. Inside the catch block, what code you wrote, compiler never checks. Even if you did not write a single line in catch, no problem to the compiler. Compiler talks about syntax only: this person provided a catch block or not.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(10000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            // empty catch is enough for the compiler
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h3>15:30 — Live: without try-catch CE; with empty catch it compiles and runs</h3>
<p>Without try-catch or throws: unreported exception, must be caught or declared to be thrown.</p>
<p>He adds try { Thread.sleep(10000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }.</p>
<p>Happily the code compiles fine. Happily the code runs fine. It sleeps 10 seconds; after 10 seconds the control stops. First way = try-catch.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(10000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
    }
}
// compiles; runs; sleeps ~10 seconds; then ends</pre>
<h3>16:30 — Board: first way by using try-catch</h3>
<p>Keep writing. He copies the try-catch version onto the board (long pause while the room writes).</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(10000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h3>17:24 — Second way: `throws` keyword</h3>
<p>Better to spell it out. Keep writing: by using `throws` keyword.</p>
<p>He still has not stated the purpose. Now he explains.</p>
<h3>18:00 — Assignment analogy: do it yourself vs delegate to a friend</h3>
<p>Small assignment: workout this program by tomorrow. In how many ways can you handle that work?</p>
<ol><li>Do the assignment on your own.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Delegate that work to your friend.</li></ol>
<p>Same two ways for the exception:</p>
<ul><li>Handle it on your own → try-catch</li></ul>
<ul><li>“Boss, I don’t want to handle; my caller is going to take care; I will delegate that responsibility to the caller” → `throws`</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// try-catch  = I handle it myself
// throws     = I delegate handling to my caller</pre>
<h3>19:16 — Purpose: delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller</h3>
<p>throws can be used to delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller. The caller may be JVM or another method.</p>
<p>Compiler asks: main, you are calling sleep, there may be a chance of checked InterruptedException, how didn’t you report, how can I compile?</p>
<p>Main answers: pass — my caller is going to take care. Ask my caller. So main keeps throws InterruptedException.</p>
<p>Who is the caller of main? JVM. If JVM is going to take care, no problem for the compiler. Happily the code compiles.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}
// compiles: main delegated InterruptedException to JVM</pre>
<h3>20:25 — Live: without `throws` CE; with `main throws InterruptedException` it compiles</h3>
<p>He removes try-catch. Thread.sleep alone: will not compile, unreported exception.</p>
<p>Then: main throws InterruptedException. Observe: happily the code compiles fine. “Must be caught or declared to be thrown” — this is the “declared to be thrown” half, using throws.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
        // CE: unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
}
class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}
// compiles</pre>
<h3>21:07 — Purpose in a single word: delegate</h3>
<p>Need of throws in a single word: to delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller. Now who is responsible to handle? The caller. In this example the caller is JVM; in the next big example the caller may be another method. Caller = method or JVM. Clear?</p>
<h3>21:38 — Board dictation: purpose of `throws` (keep writing)</h3>
<p>We can use a throws keyword to delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller (it may be another method or JVM). Then the caller method is responsible to handle that exception.</p>
<pre class='code'>// throws = delegate exception-handling responsibility to the caller
// caller = another method  OR  JVM
// after throws, that caller is responsible to handle</pre>
<h3>23:19 — Board example of `throws InterruptedException`</h3>
<p>“Better to use full form; don’t spell out I — you may feel Internet Explorer or something.” Write throws InterruptedException.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}</pre>
<p>This is the purpose / need of throws: delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller. Caller may be JVM or another method — that big example in a few minutes. Terminology clear.</p>
<h3>24:18 — Conclusions about `throws` (listen carefully)</h3>
<p>Conclusion 1 — purpose: throws is to delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller.</p>
<p>Conclusion 2 — checked only: throws is required only for checked exceptions. Correct or not? If it is checked, the compiler raises CE; to handle that we use throws. Usage of throws for unchecked exceptions is meaningless / there is no use. If you want you can use, but there is no impact, because for unchecked the compiler never asks “unreported exception…”. That CE is possible only for checked. So: required only for checked; for unchecked, no impact.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(10 / 0);
        // compiles: ArithmeticException is unchecked
        // RE: Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero
    }
}
class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws ArithmeticException {
        System.out.println(10 / 0);
        // still compiles — throws on unchecked has no impact
        // still RE: ArithmeticException: / by zero
    }
}</pre>
<h3>25:36 — Conclusion 3: only to convince the compiler</h3>
<p>If you are not using throws (for a checked case): compile-time error.</p>
<p>If you are using throws: no compile-time error.</p>
<p>So throws is required only to convince the compiler.</p>
<h3>26:07 — If `InterruptedException` really rises at runtime, who handles?</h3>
<p>Assume at runtime InterruptedException really rises. Who is responsible? You used throws, so JVM is responsible. If JVM handles: is it normal termination or abnormal? Abnormal termination.</p>
<p>Even though you used throws, termination is abnormal. Usage of `throws` keyword doesn’t prevent abnormal termination of the program.</p>
<p>Every time throws throws throws — at last who handles? JVM. JVM “handle” here means abnormal termination.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
        // compiles (compiler is convinced)
        // if InterruptedException really occurs at runtime:
        //   JVM default handler → abnormal termination
    }
}</pre>
<h3>26:53 — Therefore: try-catch is recommended over `throws`</h3>
<p>That’s why recommended to use try-catch instead of `throws`.</p>
<ul><li>try-catch → if the exception occurs, normal termination (you handled it)</li></ul>
<ul><li>throws → if the exception really occurs at runtime, abnormal termination</li></ul>
<p>throws is only to convince the compiler. At runtime if that exception occurs, program termination is always abnormal. throws doesn’t prevent abnormal termination. There may be a chance of abnormal termination even though we used throws.</p>
<p>Three or four conclusions covered.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(10000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            // handled here → if it occurs, still normal termination
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h3>27:38 — Board: the conclusions (keep writing)</h3>
<p>Main purpose: delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller. Then caller is responsible to handle.</p>
<p>Next: throws required only for checked exceptions. Usage of throws for unchecked exceptions: no use or no impact.</p>
<p>Next: throws required only to convince the compiler. And usage of throws doesn’t prevent abnormal termination of the program.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board conclusions (throws / throws clause):
// 1. delegate handling to the caller (method or JVM)
// 2. required only for checked; unchecked → no impact
// 3. only to convince compiler; does NOT prevent abnormal termination</pre>
<p>That’s all — various conclusions related to throws.</p>
<h3>30:05 — Big picture: impact of `throws` with three methods</h3>
<p>Listen carefully. Class Test. main calls doStuff. Inside doStuff, call doMoreStuff. Inside doMoreStuff, Thread.sleep(10000).</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        doStuff();
    }
    public static void doStuff() {
        doMoreStuff();
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
        // CE: unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
}</pre>
<h3>31:41 — Will this compile? No — problem is at `doMoreStuff`</h3>
<p>main calls doStuff, doStuff calls doMoreStuff, sleep is inside doMoreStuff. Ask the compiler: compile or not? No.</p>
<p>Compiler asks: `doMoreStuff`, you are calling `sleep`. There may be a chance of `InterruptedException`. Where are you handling? Without handling how can I compile — it is a checked exception. Here the problem will come.</p>
<p>Exact error: unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException must be caught or declared to be thrown.</p>
<h3>32:52 — `doMoreStuff` has two options</h3>
<ol><li>Keep the method call / sleep inside try-catch</li></ol>
<ol><li>Delegate that responsibility to the caller</li></ol>
<p>What this person did: “My caller take care. Why I have to worry?” So doMoreStuff keeps throws InterruptedException.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        doStuff();
    }
    public static void doStuff() {
        doMoreStuff();
        // CE: unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}</pre>
<h3>33:13 — After `doMoreStuff throws`, compile or not? Still no</h3>
<p>Compiler identifies who is the caller of `doMoreStuff`? → doStuff.</p>
<p>doStuff, you are calling doMoreStuff. doMoreStuff delegated InterruptedException. Where are you handling? Compile-time error moves to `doStuff`.</p>
<h3>34:10 — `doStuff` also has two options</h3>
<p>Keep this method call inside try-catch, or: “Someone raised the exception; why I have to handle? My caller is going to take care.” So doStuff also keeps throws InterruptedException.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        doStuff();
        // CE: unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
    public static void doStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        doMoreStuff();
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Compile or not? No. Caller of doStuff is `main`. Compiler goes to main: you are calling doStuff, doStuff throws IE, where are you handling?</p>
<h3>35:04 — `main` also throws; caller of `main` is JVM — now it compiles</h3>
<p>“Someone raised the exception; what is the need of handling? My caller is going to take care.” main also keeps throws InterruptedException.</p>
<p>Will it compile? Caller of main is JVM. JVM can handle because the default exception handler is already there. Happily the code compiles.</p>
<p>Different levels of delegation: doMoreStuff delegated to doStuff → doStuff delegated to main → main delegated to JVM. That is the purpose of throws: delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller. Now caller is responsible to handle.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        doStuff();
    }
    public static void doStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        doMoreStuff();
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}
// compiles: three-level delegation, last caller = JVM</pre>
<p>If you are not taking throws at a level, CE at that level. Once you took throws there, no problem at that level.</p>
<h3>36:01 — Live coding: CE at line 13, then it walks up</h3>
<p>He types main → doStuff(); doStuff → doMoreStuff(); doMoreStuff → Thread.sleep. Compile or not? At which line?</p>
<p>Line 13 — Thread.sleep. Unreported exception must be caught or declared to be thrown, at that line.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {                                          // 1
    public static void main(String[] args) {          // 2
        doStuff();                                    // 5
    }                                                 // 6
    public static void doStuff() {                    // 7
        doMoreStuff();                                // 9
    }                                                 // 10
    public static void doMoreStuff() {                // 11
        Thread.sleep(10000);                          // 13  CE here first
    }
}</pre>
<p>(He numbers the sleep line as 13 in his editor.)</p>
<h3>37:10 — `doMoreStuff throws InterruptedException` → CE moves to line 9</h3>
<p>He adds throws InterruptedException on doMoreStuff. Compile? No. Which line now? Line 9 — the call of doMoreStuff, because it delegated but the caller is not handling. Same unreported-exception CE at line 9.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        doStuff();
    }
    public static void doStuff() {
        doMoreStuff(); // CE at line 9
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}</pre>
<h3>37:44 — `doStuff throws` → CE moves to line 5 (`main`’s call)</h3>
<p>doStuff delegated to main. main is responsible. CE at line 5: unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException must be caught or declared to be thrown.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        doStuff(); // CE at line 5
    }
    public static void doStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        doMoreStuff();
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}</pre>
<h3>38:18 — `main throws` too: compiles; sleeps 10 seconds; no real interrupt</h3>
<p>For main also he keeps throws InterruptedException. Compile? Yes. Compile is fine. Run: there is a possibility, not a rule that the exception will be raised. Happily 10 seconds it sleeps, then the program terminates.</p>
<p>In this program how many threads? Only main thread. Is another thread going to interrupt? No such possibility. But for syntax, to convince the compiler, throws is required. Big-picture need of throws. Take this example.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        doStuff();
    }
    public static void doStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        doMoreStuff();
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}
// compiles; runs; sleeps ~10s; ends normally
// (IE does not actually occur — only one thread)</pre>
<h3>39:07 — Board copy of the three-level example (long writing pause)</h3>
<p>Keep writing. main throws InterruptedException calls doStuff. doStuff throws InterruptedException calls doMoreStuff. doMoreStuff throws InterruptedException does Thread.sleep(10000).</p>
<p>He first writes doMoreStuff without throws and reminds: CE, unreported exception. Then the completed picture with all three throws.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        doStuff();
    }
    public static void doStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        doMoreStuff();
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}</pre>
<h3>41:20 — Recap: different levels of delegation</h3>
<p>This example provides a clear picture.</p>
<ul><li>doMoreStuff delegated responsibility to doStuff</li></ul>
<ul><li>doStuff delegated responsibility to main</li></ul>
<ul><li>main delegated responsibility to JVM</li></ul>
<p>Different levels of delegation. Completed? Next one.</p>
<h3>42:05 — Trap: strip `throws` from `doStuff` and `doMoreStuff`; leave it only on `main`</h3>
<p>Small change. You have to decide the output. Same example. He removes throws from doMoreStuff and from doStuff. Only `main` has `throws`. Compile or not?</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        doStuff();
    }
    public static void doStuff() {
        doMoreStuff();
    }
    public static void doMoreStuff() {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
        // CE: unreported exception java.lang.InterruptedException
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
}</pre>
<p>How many people feeling yes? How many feeling no? Make sure.</p>
<h3>43:19 — Compiler: I saw `main` can handle, but you did not **delegate** to `main`</h3>
<p>He asks the compiler to compile. Compiler: I cannot. Inside doMoreStuff you are calling sleep; chance of InterruptedException; where are you handling?</p>
