class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        m1();
    }
    public static void m1() throws Exception {
        m2();
    }
    public static void m2() throws Exception {
        Thread.sleep(10000);
    }
}
// compiles: every level delegated</pre>
<p>If only main is there: no problem, happily compiles. Here multiple levels of methods: every level we have to keep `throws`. Clear?</p>
<h3>48:31 — Recap of purpose + conclusions (before the board summary)</h3>
<p>Purpose of throws? Delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller. Then caller is responsible. Caller may be a method or JVM. Point one.</p>
<p>Second: required only for checked. Usage for unchecked: no impact / no use.</p>
<p>Next: required only to convince compiler. Usage does not prevent abnormal termination. That’s why try-catch vs `throws`: try-catch is recommended when compared with throws.</p>
<p>Let me summarize all these points so you can be aware much clearly.</p>
<h3>49:22 — Board summary: `throws` keyword **or** `throws` clause</h3>
<p>Any word you can use: throws keyword or throws clause. Listen this point well.</p>
<p>First point: we can use to delegate responsibility of exception handling to the caller (it may be a method or JVM).</p>
<p>Second: it is required only for checked exceptions, and usage of throws for unchecked exceptions: there is no impact.</p>
<p>Third: it is required only to convince the compiler, and usage of throws doesn’t prevent abnormal termination of the program.</p>
<p>He spells convince: C-O-N-V-I-N-C-E. Cross-check spelling.</p>
<pre class='code'>// throws keyword / throws clause — three conclusions on the board:
// 1. delegate handling to the caller (method or JVM)
// 2. required only for checked; unchecked → no impact
// 3. only to convince compiler; does not prevent abnormal termination</pre>
<p>That’s why try-catch vs throws: try-catch is recommended. Take these conclusions.</p>
<h3>52:54 — Room copies the three conclusions (long dictation)</h3>
<p>He repeats the three paragraphs so they can copy slowly: delegate to the caller (method or JVM); required only for checked, unchecked no impact; only to convince compiler, doesn’t prevent abnormal termination of the program. Music / writing pause. Completed? Hope clear.</p>
<h3>55:34 — Extra note on the board: try-catch over `throws`</h3>
<p>Note. Note. Note.</p>
<p>It is recommended to use try-catch over `throws` keyword.</p>
<pre class='code'>// recommended: try-catch
// not recommended as the only strategy: throws (JVM → abnormal termination)</pre>
<p>That’s all for the purpose of throws. But there are multiple conclusions / multiple cases. Almost four cases. One or two very easy; two cases a bit difficult — take special care.</p>
<h3>56:40 — Case 1: `throws` on class vs constructor vs method</h3>
<p>Keep writing. Case one. Class Test, it is a constructor, and it is a method. He writes throws Exception on all three and asks: among these, which are valid and which are invalid?</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test throws Exception {          // CE: throws not allowed at class level
    Test() throws Exception {          // valid: constructor may declare throws
    }
    public void m1() throws Exception { // valid: method may declare throws
    }
}</pre>
<h3>57:38 — Why class-level `throws` is invalid</h3>
<p>A method we can use throws — no problem. We can use throws for the constructor also — no problem (even in OOPS they covered this somewhere).</p>
<p>We can call a method — then caller is responsible to handle. We can call a constructor — caller of the constructor is responsible. Can we call a class? No one is going to call a class. Class members we can call, but not the class. That’s why `throws` for the class is invalid.</p>
<p>Where can we use throws? Method level, constructor level: valid. Class level: invalid.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() throws Exception {
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Test();
        // CE: unreported exception java.lang.Exception
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
        // (constructor declared throws; caller of new Test() must handle)
    }
}
class Test {
    public void m1() throws Exception {
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Test().m1();
        // CE: unreported exception java.lang.Exception
        //     must be caught or declared to be thrown
    }
}</pre>
<h3>58:38 — Board: case 1 rule (keep writing)</h3>
<p>We can use a throws keyword for methods and constructors, but not for classes.</p>
<h3>59:12 — Case 1 example on the board</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test throws Exception {           // invalid (class level)
    Test() throws Exception {           // valid
    }
    public void m1() throws Exception { // valid
    }
}</pre>
<p>At method level, constructor level we can use throws; for class level we can’t use. Hope case one is clear.</p>
<h3>59:55 — Case 2: `throws Test` where `Test` is a normal class</h3>
