<h3>1:26:14 — Why `catch (IOException)` is CE</h3>
<p>Inside try you wrote catch IOException. When do we get IOException? If we are performing any IO operations. Inside the try block, am I performing any IO? No. Never. In try there is no chance of raising IOException. If there is no chance of raising an exception, then we can’t write a catch block for that. Unnecessary catch — we are wasting.</p>
<p>Exact CE:</p>
<p>exception java.io.IOException is never thrown in body of corresponding try statement</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.IOException;

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
        } catch (IOException e) {
        }
        // CE: exception java.io.IOException is never thrown
        //     in body of corresponding try statement
    }
}</pre>
<p>Repeat: within try, if there is no chance of raising an exception, then it’s never recommended to write catch for that. By mistake if you write catch, CE: exception … is never thrown in body of corresponding try statement.</p>
<h3>1:27:59 — This rule is **only for fully checked** exceptions</h3>
<p>Make sure: this rule is applicable only for fully checked exceptions.</p>
<ul><li>IOException — fully checked or not? Fully checked</li></ul>
<ul><li>InterruptedException — fully checked</li></ul>
<ul><li>Error — unchecked</li></ul>
<ul><li>ArithmeticException — unchecked</li></ul>
<ul><li>Exception — partially checked</li></ul>
<p>Clarity: inside try, if there is no chance of raising an exception, we can’t write catch for that exception, otherwise CE “never thrown in body of corresponding try statement” — but this rule is applicable only for fully checked exceptions.</p>
<h3>1:29:05 — Same story for `InterruptedException`</h3>
<p>Fully checked? Yes. When do we get it? If we are using `sleep` / `wait` / `join`. Am I using any such methods in this try? No. No chance of raising InterruptedException in the try. Then we cannot write catch for that. CE:</p>
<p>exception java.lang.InterruptedException is never thrown in body of corresponding try statement</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
        // CE: exception java.lang.InterruptedException is never thrown
        //     in body of corresponding try statement
    }
}</pre>
<p>If the try did call sleep, catch of InterruptedException is valid (that was the first way at 14:20):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(10000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
        // compiles: sleep can throw InterruptedException
    }
}</pre>
<h3>1:30:00 — Board dictation of the case-4 rule (keep writing)</h3>
<p>Within the try block, if there is no chance of raising an exception, then we can’t write catch block for that exception. Otherwise we will get compile-time error saying:</p>
<p>exception XXX is never thrown in body of corresponding try statement</p>
<p>But this rule is applicable only for fully checked exceptions.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Case 4 board rule:
// try has no chance of X
//   AND X is fully checked
//   → CE: exception X is never thrown in body of corresponding try statement
// unchecked / partially checked → catch is allowed even if try cannot throw it</pre>
