<h3>Board recap (his own summary, not a generic list)</h3>
<ol><li>If a checked exception can rise: handle with try-catch or `throws`, else unreported exception X must be caught or declared to be thrown.</li></ol>
<ol><li>throws delegates handling to the caller (next method or JVM).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Required only for checked; unchecked throws has no impact.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Exists only to convince the compiler; does not prevent abnormal termination → prefer try-catch.</li></ol>
<ol><li>In a call chain, every level must throws (or catch); throws only on main is not enough if lower methods never delegate.</li></ol>
<ol><li>throws on methods and constructors, not on classes.</li></ol>
<ol><li>throws only for Throwable types (incompatible types otherwise).</li></ol>
<ol><li>throw new Exception() → CE (checked). throw new Error() → compiles, then RE.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Catch of a fully checked type is illegal if try cannot throw it: exception X is never thrown in body of corresponding try statement. Unchecked and partially checked (Exception) do not get that CE.</li></ol>
